{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall be altogether joyful.</b> Now was it not already stated, ״ושמחת בחגך״ — 'and you shall rejoice in your festival'? Rather, it teaches that one should rejoice throughout all seven days. This is the meaning of אך — that is, until twenty-one days of Tishrei have passed; and after these twenty-one days, the seven days of Sukkot have already passed." "<b>It is also possible</b> that it comes to hint that one should diminish the joy of this world, for the term אך is a term of diminution. And because one has already rejoiced on the festival of Pesach, which is the beginning of the omer harvest, and his joy grew greater on the festival of harvest, as it is written (Isaiah 9:2): ״שמחו לפניך כשמחת בקציר״ — 'they rejoice before You as with joy at the harvest' — now on the festival of ingathering, when one gathers in his grain and his fruit, his wool and his flax, his oil and his drink, and rest and tranquility come to him after the toil and trouble and labor — behold, his joy is doubled. Therefore Scripture mentions here, on the festival of Sukkot, ״והיית אך שמח״ — 'and you shall be altogether joyful' — in order to diminish the joy, for indeed all forms of אכין ורקין (אך and רק) are terms of restriction. And Scripture teaches you that a person should conduct himself in the joy of this world in a moderate way. And even at the time of performing the commandments, it is fitting for him to rejoice and to tremble, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Psalms 2:11): ״עבדו את ה' ביראה וגילו ברעדה״ — 'Serve HaShem with fear, and rejoice with trembling' — wherever there is גילה (rejoicing), let there be רעדה (trembling) there, because in this world the evil inclination exists, and it is drawn after joy, and the righteous are not secure in themselves. But the essence of joy and its perfection is in the World to Come, in the vision of the Shekhinah's presence, in an abundance that has no interruption, and with added apprehension and delight that have no end. And so Isaiah of blessed memory said (Isaiah 29:19): ״ויספו ענוים בה' שמחה ואביוני אדם בקדוש ישראל יגילו״ — 'And the humble shall increase their joy in HaShem, and the needy among humankind shall exult in the Holy One of Israel.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:50.272108935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}